ke to do better  uh but that the once  uh you start looking at igg  which is after three or four weeks of  infection and  this system was performing pretty well  uh and that was  no firm conclusions were made at that  other than highlighting these points  now one of the things that became  obvious  um during this time frame was that  there's a lot of  energetic cross reactivity in  the standard two-tier assays  and this is just a a list of some of the  worst ones and if you look at  um the flagellum which is  at 41 kilodons  there are studies that show up to uh  40 or more of normal healthy individuals  with no history of lung disease have  uh antibody against 41. uh  that the the uh antigen at 60  uh also was highly cross-reactive  uh that the an antigen at uh  30 kilo domes uh was uh is homologous to  uh an antigen uh found commonly in gram  negative bacteria  and uh also there were studies that came  out that demonstrated that  um the wholesale sonicates  uh when used as first-year assays  uh had a tremendous amount of  cross reactivity in tropical regions  where there were no lyme disease  so that was just some things that were  highlighted  uh during that time frame if you look at  flagellum if you look at the epitope  mapping  you quickly see that there are  variable sequences and conserved regions  and domains that are semi-conserved  so there's areas that are  really quite cross-reactive with all  other bacterial flagellums  but there are there's at least one  epitope  in 41 which is specific more specific to  borrelia and  that was something that we noted in my  research group  and have focused on  looking for more specific epitopes  within uh these individual proteins  if uh one of the bands on  the western blot is at 66 and we  looked at uh that and we found that it  was  very highly cross-reactive and this  slide is taken from the paper that's uh  referenced here and um  so when you start to think about things  if you're looking at a western blot well  y